{{also|Disciple}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|disciple}}, {{m|enm|discipul}}, from {{inh|en|ang|discipul||disciple, scholar}}, from {{der|en|la|discipulus||pupil, learner}}. Later influenced or superseded in Middle English by {{cog|fro|deciple}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈsaɪpl̩/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-disciple.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{hyph|en|dis|ci|ple}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A person who learns from another, especially one who then teaches others.
# An active [[follower]] or [[adherent]] of someone, or some [[philosophy]] etc.
#* {{RQ:KJV|Matthew|9|10}}
#*: And it came to pass, as Jesus sat at meat in the house, behold, many publicans and sinners came and sat down with him and his '''disciples'''.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|author={{w|Ben Travers}}
|chapter=4|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1521052W A Cuckoo in the Nest]
|passage=By some paradoxical evolution rancour and intolerance have been established in the vanguard of primitive Christianity. Mrs. Spoker, in common with many of the stricter '''disciples''' of righteousness, was as inclement in demeanour as she was cadaverous in aspect.}}
# {{lb|en|Ireland}} A wretched, miserable-looking man.

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|student}}

